  "text": "The wobble‑induced torques acting on the planet’s inner metallic region and its solid outer shell can cause a slight difference in their spin rates. How large this differential spin becomes is controlled by two factors: the resistance that comes from the shape of the interface between the two layers, and the drag created by the shearing motion of the fluid at that interface. The analysis shows that the drag produced by a thin, turbulent, magnetically active boundary layer is the dominant factor limiting the relative motion. By inserting realistic values for the overall shape, density and electrical conductivity of the inner region, the model predicts magnetic field strengths, fluid flow speeds and characteristic time scales that agree well with the measurements that are currently available."
